ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085516 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085516 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f03f76b8-d043-505d-8492-b243b8108a8a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085517 #edb/ref 1080 "e^{4x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085517 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085517 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "03978ae7-6007-56f1-bded-7c38d20b4b66" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085518 #edb/ref 1080 "e^{x^{2}/4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085518 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085518 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "102ccfd1-32b7-5263-903f-1e88f2bef7f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085519 #edb/ref 1080 "x^{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085519 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186085519 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "272829e1-df88-5dc5-b413-aa305e1b1b85" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087452 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21741" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087452 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y = y(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + 2y = 2e^{-2x}\n$$\n\nWrite the equation in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y I(x) \\right) = I(x)Q(x),$ where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087452 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1e3c0253-226d-596c-a831-7c68ec76b21c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087452 #edb/ref 1074 "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x) = 2$ and $Q(x) = 2e^{-2x}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 2\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x) = e^{2x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y) & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{-2x} \\\\ {e}^{2x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{2x}\\cdot y & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y I(x)\\right){:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{2x}) & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087453 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21742" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087453 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the general solution of the following differential equation using the method of integrating factors.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac {3} {x} y = \\dfrac{\\cos x}{x^2}, \\qquad x \\gt 0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087453 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "89446fd7-75c2-5da5-bd97-469100f3ca7b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087453 #edb/ref 1074 "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x) = \\dfrac{3}{x}$ and $Q(x) = \\dfrac{\\cos x}{x^2}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (3/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x) = x^3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{x}y) & ={x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{x}^{2}\\cdot y & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y\\cdot I(x)\\right)$ and carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{3}) & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{3})\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{3} & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this integral, we integrate by parts. \n\n$$\n\\int uv'\\,\\textrm d x = uv - \\int u' v\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nLet $u = x, u' = 1, v' = \\cos x,$ and $v = \\sin x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y{x}^{3} & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{3} & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{3} & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087454 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21743" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087454 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the following initial value problem using the method of integrating factors.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + 3x^2y = 9x^2, \\qquad y(0) = 1\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087454 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "09d09b0b-2132-59b2-9eb9-ae0748f09252" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087454 #edb/ref 1074 "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith $P(x) = 3x^2$ and $Q(x) = 9x^2.$\n\nWe compute an integrating factor using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x) = e^{x^3}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{x}^{2}y) & ={e}^{{x}^{3}}\\cdot 9{x}^{2} \\\\ {e}^{{x}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\cdot y & =9{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{{x}^{3}}) & =9{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x,$ making use of the substitution $u = x^3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{{x}^{3}})\\text{d}x & =\\int 9{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{e}^{{x}^{3}} & =\\int 3{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ y{e}^{{x}^{3}} & =3{e}^{u}+C \\\\ y{e}^{{x}^{3}} & =3{e}^{{x}^{3}}+C \\\\ y & =3+C{e}^{-{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the general solution. To find the value of $C$ corresponding to the specific solution, we apply the initial condition $y(0) = 1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =3+C{e}^{-{0}^{3}} \\\\ 1 & =3+C \\\\ C & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = 3 - 2e^{-x^3}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087877 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087877 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087877 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049443 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087877 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087877 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088059 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087877 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087877 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087877 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284738 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087877 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186311565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087877 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087877 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087877 #edb/ref 1039 "Transforming First-Order Linear ODEs Using Integrating Factors" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087877 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186087452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087877 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "9c49e068-198f-59ab-8835-7018b02d7d0c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087878 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087878 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087878 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088140 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087878 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186088142 #e