ref 17592186088819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087942 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087942 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087942 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186088822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087942 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087942 #edb/ref 1045 "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Integrating Factors" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087942 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186088817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087942 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186087942 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "115bfa9d-34ae-51ca-aa04-b0f23cf266a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088144 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186085509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088144 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186085515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088144 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335808" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088144 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem.\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} + \\frac{x}{2} \\cdot y = x, y(0) = 1$\nSelect the correct options below. An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x) =$ {{field-1}}.\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is $y(x) =$ {{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088144 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8d9c75e9-1ee8-5d41-904e-f9bb375cf039" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088817 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088817 #edb/ref 1061 597598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088817 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2aeafed5-8ce5-5873-95fe-baefd4b2fe7b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088817 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088818 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088818 #edb/ref 1061 597599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088818 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "552b03ae-2839-592c-a5fa-667890639337" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088818 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088819 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088819 #edb/ref 1061 597600 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088819 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0c98b113-16b4-5f8c-8b8c-a5ffbbbd075d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088819 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088820 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088820 #edb/ref 1061 597603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088820 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6c426f6e-80e3-59e6-8472-9394bf844946" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088820 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088821 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186088822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088821 #edb/ref 1061 597607 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088821 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8f99a521-6d0e-5370-b62d-331c98f13937" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088821 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186087879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088822 #edb/ref 1061 597611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088822 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ad072f0e-cc4b-5f68-b499-63509c901c8a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088822 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186088861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088859 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that a first-order linear ODE can always be expressed in the standard form\n\n$$\ny'(x)+P(x)y(x) =Q(x).\n$$\n\nFirst-order linear ODEs are special because we can always find the general solution using an **integrating factor.** \n\nAn integrating factor is a function $I(x)$ that allows us to rewrite the left-hand side as an exact derivative after multiplying the equation by $I(x).$ The resulting equation can then be solved by integrating both sides.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the following first-order linear ODE.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\frac{2}{x}\\, y = 3\n$$\n\nAs it stands, this differential equation cannot be integrated directly. However, if we multiply both sides of the equation by $I(x) = x^2,$ we get\n\n$$\nx^2\\cdot \\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + x^2\\cdot \\frac{2}{x}\\, y = x^2\\cdot 3\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as follows:\n\n$$\nx^2 \\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + 2 x y = 3x^2 \\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nNow, here's the neat part. Provided our integrating factor is correct, we can *always* write the left-hand side of the equation as the exact derivative\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}\\left(y\\cdot I(x)\\right)\n$$\n\nwhich in our case is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}\\left(y \\cdot x^2\\right).\n$$\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}( y \\cdot x^2) = 3x^2.\n$$\n\nWe can now find the general solution by integrating both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot {x}^{2}) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot {x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y\\cdot {x}^{2} & ={x}^{3}+C \\\\ y=x+\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can check that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}( y \\cdot x^2) = 3x^2\n$$\n\nis indeed equivalent to the original equation $(\\ast)$ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot {x}^{2}) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y)\\cdot {x}^{2}+y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y\\cdot 2x & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy & =3{x}^{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy finding an appropriate integrating factor, we can solve any first-order linear ODE! So the question is, how do we find an integrating factor? Let's discuss this next." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088859 #edb/ref 1065 14550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088859 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1066a4d7-d790-5316-9925-1ac0e928ce01" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088859 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088860 #edb/ref 1064 "Given a first-order linear ODE, we can find an integrating factor and use it to solve the equation using the following procedure.\n\n- **Step 1**: Write the equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\n- **Step 2**: Compute an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition\n\n$$\nI(x) = e^{\\int P(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x} \\,.\n$$\n\n- **Step 3**: Multiply both sides of the equation found in step 1 by $I(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nI(x)\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + I(x)P(x)y = I(x)Q(x)\n$$\n\n- **Step 4**: Rewrite the left side as \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\Bigl(y I(x)\\Bigr)\n$$\n\nIf steps 1 through 3 are done correctly, then we can always do this, and we can check that it's correct by differentiating. Our new equation is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\Bigl(y I(x)\\Bigr) = I(x)Q(x)\n$$\n\n- **Step 5**: Integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x$ and solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yI(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int I(x)Q(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ yI(x) & =\\int I(x)Q(x)\\,\\text{d}x+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{I(x)}(\\int I(x)Q(x)\\,\\text{d}x+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nYou might be wondering where the formula for $I(x)$ comes from; we'll discuss this at the end of the lesson. First, let's apply this method to a concrete example in the next slide." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088860 #edb/ref 1065 14551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088860 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a6a381ed-e9ab-5deb-9d74-30900610edf1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088860 #edb/ref 1067 "The Integrating Factor Method" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088861 #edb/ref 1064 "We will now derive the formula for the integrating factor. \n\nConsider the first-order linear equation in standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x).\n$$\n\nOur goal is to rewrite the left-hand side as the derivative of a product.\n\nTo do that, we multiply both sides of the equation by some function $I(x)$ that we wish to determine.\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}I(x)\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + {\\color{purple}I(x)P(x)}y} = I(x)Q(x)\n$$\n\nWe want the left-hand side to match the derivative of $y\\cdot I(x)$. But by the product rule,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\Bigl(y\\cdot I(x)\\Bigr)\n=\n{\\color{blue}I(x)\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + {\\color{purple}I'(x)}y}.\n$$\n\nSo, if we choose $I(x)$ so that\n\n$$\nI'(x) = I(x)P(x),\n$$\n\nthen the left-hand side becomes $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\Bigl(y\\cdot I(x)\\Bigr).$\n\nDividing both sides of $I'(x) = I(x)P(x)$ by $I(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{I'(x)}{I(x)} = P(x).\n$$\n\nNext, integrating both sides with respect to $x$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{I}^{'}(x)}{I(x)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}(I(x)))\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}(I(x)) & =\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x+C \\\\ I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x+C} \\\\  & ={e}^{C}\\cdot {e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, because multiplying $I(x)$ by a nonzero constant does not change the method, we can choose $e^C = 1$ and define an integrating factor as\n\n$$\nI(x) = e^{\\int P(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088861 #edb/ref 1065 14640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088861 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a7f8aa2e-e022-57eb-be82-5f539a4512f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186088861 #edb/ref 1067 "Deriving the Integrating Factor" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309024 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{3}{x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309024 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309024 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "24c590c3-891b-511b-9e8d-1e0317eac036" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309150 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{{e}^{x}+C}{{x}^{3}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309150 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309150 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "34e87914-2491-5ea9-986c-2fa933b9f42e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309928 #edb/ref 1080 "{x}^{2}({e}^{x}+C)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309928 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309928 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "93802e8a-2438-526b-9013-756c85306841" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310322 #edb/ref 1080 "{x}^{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310322 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310322 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c4250262-bc93-5a7e-a34a-2623ae9d7561" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310355 #edb/ref 1080 "{e}^{3/x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310355 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310355 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c82f0da4-6a92-5001-89a2-66f34618a0ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310385 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{{e}^{x}+C}{{x}^{2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310385 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310385 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "cc68d1a8-cb75-5053-8d7d-c7bbccf33bcd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310405 #edb/ref 1080 "{x}^{3}({e}^{x}+C)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310405 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310405 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ce5f5849-731e-572b-9350-7951b8593415" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310646 #edb/ref 1080 "{e}^{{x}^{3}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310646 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310646 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ee20a83a-6373-5864-bf59-eeaad3c5fb28" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310888 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186309024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310888 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186310322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310888 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186310355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310888 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186310646 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310888 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310888 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310888 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186310322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310888 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "26833fad-1674-5264-a99a-1ab167ee5815" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311159 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186309150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311159 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186309928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311159 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186310385 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311159 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186310405 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311159 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311159 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311159 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186309150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311159 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "842dfce6-3596-5e08-aa3d-f4ed3e02c6ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311587 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311587 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186311159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311587 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335412" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311587 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311587 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{x}y=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{3}},\\,x>0.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.$\\,$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is given by $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311587 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "039312c7-7497-5736-bf10-d497d1c61328" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311587 #edb/ref 1074 "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=\\frac{3}{x}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{3}}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (3/x)\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)={x}^{3}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{x}y) & ={x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{x}^{2}\\cdot y & ={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x))$ and carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dx}(y{x}^{3})\\,dx & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{3} & ={e}^{x}+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{{e}^{x}+C}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345637 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{2})={x}^{2}{e}^{x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345637 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186345637 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "205e27b5-4aef-53eb-8cf0-e7365d5f4c57" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346382 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{x})={e}^{x}{e}^{x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346382 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346382 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7510063a-6cf7-5663-bf6a-5afc7e2ed0eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346505 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{-x})={e}^{-x}{e}^{x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346505 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346505 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8483449c-5ecd-5c41-92e1-0ded8b81ec2c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346964 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(yx)=x{e}^{x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346964 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186346964 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b9c267a8-32e4-55c8-8af3-388216c0c46a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186347167 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot {e}^{x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] 